==============================================================================
experiment-head-reference -- match against only what the benchmark resolved
2026-10-31   normalizer v1-3e7b9f9d59bbb19e   script dev/experiment-head-reference.R
==============================================================================
QUESTION as posed: take the cases the recent test cleared YES, treat those as
the database, run a large batch against that group only, and see whether new
YES cases surface.

MINI REFERENCE: the BMF rows for every EIN that BASELINE-2026-10C's augmented
arm cleared YES. 2,979 YES identities over 2,813 distinct EINs, 9,253 reference
rows -- 0.2385% of the 3,880,431-row production reference. Carved out of the
production bundle rather than rebuilt, so the keys are byte-identical and the
comparison measures competition rather than normalization.

WHAT A SUBSET REFERENCE CAN DO, which decides how to read everything below.
Every row of the mini reference is also in the production one, so it cannot
reach an EIN the full reference could not, and cannot answer a query whose true
EIN is outside the 2,813. It produces a YES the full reference did not for
exactly one reason: the competitors are gone and the margin gate sees an
uncontested top candidate. That is either the full reference's MAYBE tier being
competition-blocked, or a false positive. Both arms report the full-reference
answer beside the mini one, because nothing else separates them.

==============================================================================
ARM R -- the benchmark's own 4,748 in-scope grant rows
==============================================================================
Replicates dev/build-baseline.R exactly: pf_read_grants ->
pf_recover_name_from_street -> pf_match_grants. Its unit is the in-scope grant
row, 4,903 grants less 155 out of scope. (An earlier attempt used pf_prepare(),
whose 4,683 recipient identities are a different unit with ids that do not
join; the cross-tab came back empty and that is why.)

  full tier \ mini tier   no candidate    YES   MAYBE     NO
  YES     2,979                     5  2,963       3      8
  MAYBE   1,044                   642     35      83    284
  NO        725                   565      2       3    155

THE RESULT WORTH HAVING: 35 MAYBE -> YES, and in 17 of them (48.6%) the mini
reference clears to THE SAME EIN the full reference already had as its own top
candidate. On those the production reference held the right answer and the
margin gate would not clear it, because competitors kept the margin tight.
That is 1.6% of the MAYBE tier, measured rather than argued, and it is direct
support for the reference-side duplicate finding -- 15.9% of the MAYBE tie band
is two BMF rows carrying one name for one body (dev/CLUSTER-FINDINGS.md 8).

The other 18 clear to a different EIN, or the full reference had no candidate.
A subset reference cannot know better than the full one, so those are for hand
review, not adoption.

NO -> YES: 2, both to an EIN the full reference did not pick. Suspect.

COST OF REMOVING THE REFERENCE, which was not expected: 16 of the 2,979 full-YES
rows (0.5%) stop clearing -- 11 drop to MAYBE or NO, 5 find no candidate at all
-- and 1 clears to a different EIN. Shrinking a reference is not monotone.

STATISTICS CONTROL (PF_EXP_STATS=mini recomputes name_freq and token_idf over
the 9,253 rows instead of carrying the production tables):

  MAYBE -> YES        35  ->  41
  ... same EIN        17  ->  19
  NO tier             155 ->  138   (17 move NO -> MAYBE)

So the inflated IDF of a tiny reference is mildly more permissive and changes
no conclusion. Production statistics is the right default; the knob exists so
the two effects are never confounded again.

==============================================================================
ARM C -- 50,000 fresh grants, the benchmark's own rows excluded
==============================================================================
50,000 grant rows -> 40,977 identities, matched in 6.2 min (0.009 s/identity,
20x faster than production: candidate generation against 9,253 rows is cheap).

  NO     38,697   94.44%
  YES     1,546    3.77%
  MAYBE     734    1.79%

So 3.77% of fresh grant identities go to one of these 2,813 organisations.
Against 2,813 of roughly 2.19M distinct recipient names in the corpus -- 0.13%
of names -- that is about 29x enrichment over uniform, which is real but far
less concentrated than the headline recipients suggest. SALVATION ARMY appears
28,096 times, but the recipient distribution as a whole is flat.

PRECISION CONTROL: 400 of the 1,546 mini-YES identities re-matched against the
full reference.

  full reference verdict:  YES 368   MAYBE 30   NO 2
  survives contest (full also YES, same EIN):  352   88.0%
  full clears a DIFFERENT EIN:                  16
  full does not clear at all:                   32
  -> 48 of 400, 12.0%, are the mini reference clearing what contest would not

So arm C's 3.77% carries about 88% precision against the full reference as the
standard: roughly 3.3% contest-surviving. Do not quote 3.77% as accuracy.

(The 2.045 s/identity in that control is a fixed-cost artifact -- 400 queries
is far too few to amortise the full reference's setup. The comparable
production figure is 0.183 s/identity.)

==============================================================================
SCALING TO THE 1,000,000-GRANT RUN
==============================================================================
  identities per grant   0.82   (40,977 / 50,000)
  1M grants           -> ~820,000 identities
  at 0.009 s/identity -> ~2.05 h matching
  plus the chunked corpus scan (~6 min) and pf_prepare
  TOTAL                  ~2.5 h
  expected YES           ~31,000 at ~88% precision

  PFMATCH_DIR=. PF_EXP_ARM=C PF_EXP_N=1000000 Rscript dev/experiment-head-reference.R

==============================================================================
THE ANSWER
==============================================================================
Yes, new YES cases surface -- about 3.8% of fresh grant identities -- but they
are new GRANTS to organisations already resolved, not new organisations. A
subset reference cannot discover an EIN it does not contain.

The finding worth keeping is not in the large batch at all. It is arm R's 17
cases where the full reference already had the right top candidate and
competition stopped it clearing. Scaling the batch tests coverage; scaling the
rerun against a bigger slice of MAYBE tests the hypothesis.
==============================================================================

==============================================================================
ADDENDUM 2026-10-31 -- the hypothesis the experiment above does NOT test
==============================================================================
The question behind the request was whether grants that fail against the BMF
might still match against grants that already cleared, because foundations
write recipient names by their own consistent convention while the BMF carries
the registered legal name.

THE ARMS ABOVE DO NOT TEST THAT. The mini reference was carved from the BMF
rows of the resolved EINs, so arm C is still grants-to-BMF, with a smaller BMF.
A grant-side reference -- the filer-written name and address of each cleared
record, carrying the EIN it resolved to -- was never built.

Measured instead, on the 369,260 cleared grant rows of the triangulation run,
which carry both the filer-written recip_name and the matched bmf_name over
31,722 EINs. Restricted to the 16,417 EINs holding two or more distinct
renderings: 312,306 rows.

EXACT HITS, per grant row -- the operational unit, since that is what arrives:

  hits another cleared GRANT rendering exactly   293,742   94.06%
  hits its own BMF name exactly                  147,290   47.16%
  grant-exact but NOT bmf-exact                  150,286   48.12%
  bmf-exact but NOT grant-exact                    3,834    1.23%
  neither                                         14,730    4.72%

That is the hypothesis's strongest evidence. 94% of cleared rows are
byte-identical to another cleared rendering of the same organisation, against
47% identical to the BMF name, and nearly half could be resolved by an exact
grant-side key where an exact BMF key fails.

FUZZY SIMILARITY, per DISTINCT rendering (Jaro-Winkler, 49,918 renderings):

  to the nearest OTHER cleared rendering    median 0.960   mean 0.936
  to its own BMF name                       median 0.967   mean 0.948
  closer to a grant than to the BMF                31.74%
  at or above the 0.95 bar: grant 60.33%, BMF 61.24%

ON FUZZY DISTANCE THE GRANT SIDE IS NOT BETTER -- it is marginally worse.

The two results are not in conflict; they are different units. The exact-hit
figure is per row and so weighted by frequency: a common rendering repeats, so
a row very likely shares its string with another row. The fuzzy figure is per
distinct rendering and unweighted, so the long tail of one-off renderings, which
has no close sibling, dominates it.

SO THE GRANT-SIDE ADVANTAGE IS IN FREQUENCY, NOT IN SIMILARITY. It will help
where a recipient is granted often, and where the filer convention is an
acronym or colloquial name the BMF legal name does not resemble -- the cases
with b_sim near zero and g_near high:

  THE YARD                 g_near 0.933   b_sim 0.000
  USO                      g_near 0.789   b_sim 0.000
  THE CFA                  g_near 0.607   b_sim 0.000
  THE ARC                  g_near 0.927   b_sim 0.423
  SOCAL KOCE TV FOUNDATION g_near 0.928   b_sim 0.500

It will not help a rare recipient with an unusual rendering, and it is not a
general claim that fuzzy matching is easier grant-side.

WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT, and it is ~15 minutes rather than hours: build the
grant-side dictionary -- (normalized recip_name, street_key, zip5) -> EIN over
the cleared rows -- and match the benchmark's 1,044 MAYBE and 725 NO rows
against it. That is the user's question stated exactly: do the cases that fail
against the BMF hit a cleared grant rendering?

THE DESIGN CONSTRAINT to carry into that test: the dictionary's EINs came from
the pipeline itself, so resolving new grants against it propagates the
pipeline's own decisions, errors included. It is a recall device whose precision
is inherited, not established, and it needs the same contest control arm C used
-- verify a sample against the BMF -- plus pf_consistency(), which exists for
this.

==============================================================================
ADDENDUM 2 -- the grant-side dictionary, run 2026-10-31
dev/experiment-grant-dictionary.R    log: scratch DICT.log
==============================================================================
Dictionary: the filer-written name and address of every cleared grant row from
the triangulation run, carrying the EIN it resolved to. 88,744 distinct
(rendering, address, EIN) rows over 31,721 EINs. 100 rows that are the
benchmark's own were excluded, so the test is not circular on the queries.

AMBIGUITY -- the "THE ARC" worry, measured and settled:

  distinct bare name keys                 63,310
  bare name keys carrying >1 EIN             727   1.15%   worst: 39 EINs
  distinct (name, zip5) keys              66,425
  (name, zip5) keys carrying >1 EIN            0   0.00%

HABITAT FOR HUMANITY alone is 39 EINs, RONALD MCDONALD HOUSE 31, BIG BROTHERS
BIG SISTERS 29. Keying on name alone is unusable; name + ZIP5 is unambiguous
across all 66,425 keys. The address is what makes the dictionary work.

BMF-reference tier x grant-dictionary tier, over the benchmark's 4,748 rows:

  BMF \ dict     no cand    YES   MAYBE     NO
  YES   2,979        975    794     152  1,058
  MAYBE 1,044        297    115     190    442
  NO      725        386     10      29    300

WHAT IT RECOVERS
  MAYBE -> YES   115 of 1,044   (11.0% of the MAYBE tier)
    agreeing with the BMF's own top candidate:  47   40.9%
    a new claim needing adjudication:           68
  NO -> YES       10 of 725     (1.4%)
    agreeing:  3    new claim:  7

  Combined 125 of 4,748 = 2.63 points of clear rate, of which the 50 agreeing
  cases are 1.05 points. Both clear the stop rule's "every remaining lever
  moves <1% of grants" bar; the trustworthy half only just.

WHAT IT CANNOT DO -- and this is decisive about how to use it. The dictionary
reproduces only 794 of the 2,979 BMF-YES rows, 26.7%. It holds 31,721 EINs
against the BMF's ~1.9M organisations, so as a REPLACEMENT reference it is far
worse. 37 BMF-YES rows clear to a DIFFERENT EIN against it.

THE DISAGREEMENTS ARE THE KNOWN AFFILIATE FAILURE, not a new one:

  YALE UNIVERSITY            -> YALE UNIVERSITY ALUMNI FUND  066078326 vs 060646973
  FLORIDA STATE UNIV FOUNDA  -> FLORIDA STATE UNIVERSITY     815312398 vs 591961248
  ANY BABY CAN               -> ANY BABY CAN                 742684333 vs 010809682
  MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING   -> MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING     131924236 vs 020597827

An organisation against its own foundation or alumni fund, and chapter networks
picking the wrong chapter. dev/AFFILIATION-FINDINGS.md and the dyad work are
about exactly this, so adjudicating these connects to existing work.

The clean recoveries are spelling and spacing the BMF name does not share:

  CITIMEALS ON WHEELS       -> CITY MEALS ON WHEELS       133634381 == 133634381
  LOCK HAVEN YMCA           -> LOCK HAVEN AREA YMCA       240798644 == 240798644
  HEALTH MEDICINE POLICY    -> HEALTH AND MEDICINE POLICY 363143826 == 363143826

VERDICT: the hypothesis holds, as a SUPPLEMENT. A grant-side dictionary is a
recovery pass to run after the BMF cascade, never instead of it, and the
agreement test is the acceptance rule -- take the cases where the dictionary
and the BMF's own top candidate concur, queue the rest.

LIMITATION to carry forward: this dictionary was built from the triangulation
run, whose population is drawn from multi-rendering addresses, so it
over-represents shared addresses and probably chapters. A dictionary built from
a corpus-representative run should be measured before the precision figures
above are trusted as general.

==============================================================================
ADDENDUM 3 -- the pass, validated end to end through the package API
2026-10-31   pf_grant_dictionary() + pf_recover_from_dictionary()
==============================================================================
Run on the benchmark population by the pipeline's normal path -- pf_prepare(),
then pf_run_batches() against REF-augmented.rds, then the recovery pass. The
unit here is the recipient IDENTITY (4,683), not the in-scope grant row
(4,748) the baseline uses, so these counts are not the 115/47 of addendum 2.

  BMF run      YES 2,865   MAYBE 973   NO 845      clear rate 61.18%
  dictionary   88,744 renderings, 31,721 EINs, 0 dropped ambiguous
  retried      1,818 unresolved identities
  cleared      106 of them
  agreeing     42 with the BMF's own top candidate   (39.6%)

  accept = "agree"   +42    61.18% -> 62.08%   +0.90 points   64 queued
  accept = "all"     +106   61.18% -> 63.44%   +2.26 points    0 queued

THE DEFAULT IS "agree" and that is the number to plan with: +0.90 points, with
64 cases going to an adjudication queue rather than into the status frame. On
the grant-row unit of addendum 2 the same rule was worth 1.05 points, so the
corroborated lever is around one point either way -- which straddles the stop
rule's "every remaining lever moves <1% of grants" bar rather than clearing it
comfortably. "all" is for measurement only: it adopts 64 claims that the BMF
reference declines to corroborate, from a dictionary this pipeline labelled.

AMBIGUITY CONFIRMED CLEAN ON REAL DATA. 0 renderings dropped across 88,744 --
no (name, zip5) key in the dictionary carries two EINs. The guard is kept
because a silent collision there is the one failure the pass must not have, and
the unit tests cover it with synthetic collisions.

ONE EDGE CASE FOUND BY THE TESTS and fixed: when every rendering is ambiguous
the dictionary empties, and np_reference() cannot canonicalize a zero-row
frame. It now stops naming the guard that emptied it instead of failing inside
npmatch.
